    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "@wence- @brandon-b-miller \n\nThank you. The issues related to CUDA synchronization or pthread synchronization have been resolved with the #18367 . Since I enabled PTDS in libcudf, now in \"CUDA API\" row of the spawned streams, **I only observe `cudaStreamSynchronize_ptsz` and not `cudaStreamSynchronize` anymore**. I understand that `cudaStreamSynchronize_ptsz` is likely the per-thread local synchronization, while `cudaStreamSynchronize` is for synchronization with the \"default stream\".\n- The positive outcome is that I can now finally launch a large number of streams without any interference \ud83d\udd25 \n\nIn conclusion regarding this issue, initially, I suspected it was a pylibcudf issue, and I also verified the libcudf itself as posted above. I was also curious about the stack trace reported by nsys. Thanks for the #18367 and I was actually making similar code changes myself. In the end, the issues with pthread synchronization & CUDA synchronization have been eliminated, and now the streams in pylibcudf exhibit independent parallelism, which is great!\n\n---\n\nI'm also a bit uncertain whether enabling RMM with PTDS will lead to this issues. I may need to study whether using RMM with PTDS generally results in performance improvements.\nInstead of getting RMM installed via conda, I could compile the RMM source code with PTDS enabled within my conda environment and then compile libcudf as I normally do. Please correct me if my understanding is incorrect.\n\n",
